 Core Transaction Logic Flows
Scenario 1: P2P Fund Transfer Workflow
Transaction Initialization:
Accept authorization payload with idempotency key: source_account, destination_account, amount.
Open the atomic boundary.
Idempotency Check:
Scan for the idempotency key in the transaction register. If it exists, return the cached result immediately to prevent duplicate charges.
Deterministic Pessimistic Locking:
Acquire explicit, exclusive row-level write locks on the two accounts.
Deadlock Prevention Rule: Always lock accounts in ascending alphabetical order of their unique primary keys (e.g., lock Account A before Account B). This prevents circular lock-waits.
Balance Verification:
Read source account available balance. Check: available_balance >= transfer_amount. If insufficient, raise an exception and exit.
Ledger Modification:
Decrement source account current_balance and available_balance.
Increment destination account current_balance and available_balance.
Increment the row_version counter on both accounts.
Journal and Audit Logging:
Write record to TRANSACTIONS with status Settled.
Write record to TRANSFER_DETAILS.
Write two rows to BALANCE_AUDIT_LOGS capturing the updated states of both accounts.
Commit Phase:
Flush all operations to the Write-Ahead Log, release row locks, and return an approval receipt.
Scenario 2: Compensating Transaction Reversal Workflow
When an external payment rail reports an unrecoverable failure after internal debit execution:
Open a new, distinct atomic transaction boundary.
Locate the original transaction record using its unique settlement reference.
Verify that the original status is Settled and has not already been reversed.
Acquire an exclusive row-level lock on the source account.
Increment the source account balance by the original transfer amount.
Append a new record to TRANSACTIONS flagged as Reversal, referencing the original transaction_id.
Write an audit log entry documenting the corrective balance change.
Commit the transaction and notify the customer.

3 Isolation Level Analysis & Concurrency Anomalies
Code
+--------------------+-------------------+------------------------+----------------------+--------------------+
| Isolation Level    | Dirty Read Risk   | Non-Repeatable Read    | Phantom Read Risk    | Lost Update Risk   |
+--------------------+-------------------+------------------------+----------------------+--------------------+
| Read Uncommitted   | High Risk         | High Risk              | High Risk            | Vulnerable         |
| Read Committed     | Fully Prevented   | Possible (Problematic) | Possible             | Vulnerable         |
| Repeatable Read    | Fully Prevented   | Fully Prevented        | Prevented via MVCC   | Prevented (Fails)  |
| Serializable       | Fully Prevented   | Fully Prevented        | Fully Prevented      | Fully Prevented    |
+--------------------+-------------------+------------------------+----------------------+--------------------+
Why Read Committed Fails for Audits: The Non-Repeatable Read Hazard
If a daily balance audit runs under the default Read Committed isolation level, it queries Account 1 (finding $1,000), pauses while a transfer moves $500 from Account 1 to Account 2, and then reads Account 2 (finding $1,500). The audit sees a combined balance of $2,500 instead of the real $2,000 total, creating a false balancing discrepancy.
The ApexPay Standard: Repeatable Read and Serializable Modes
General Transfers: Execute under Repeatable Read using snapshot isolation to ensure consistent balance checks throughout the transaction without reading uncommitted shifts from concurrent processes.
Ledger Audits and Close-of-Day: Execute strictly under Serializable isolation via Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI), which aborts and rolls back any transaction that exhibits anomalous read/write dependency cycles.

4 Concurrency Control Implementation Mechanisms
Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) Mechanics
ApexPay utilizes an MVCC database engine. When a transaction updates an account balance, the engine does not overwrite existing data pages in place. Instead:
It writes a new version of the row marked with the current transaction's identifier.
It flags the older row version with an expiry marker matching the same transaction ID.
Concurrent read queries continue reading the older row version without pausing or locking. Readers never block writers, and writers never block readers.
Code
Row Versioning Mechanics:
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| Row Version 1 (Account 101): Balance: $500.00 | Valid: Txn 100 to 205                   |
| Row Version 2 (Account 101): Balance: $400.00 | Valid: Txn 205 to Inactive (Current)    |
|                                                                                         |
| Concurrent Read Query (Txn 202) -> Reads Version 1 (Sees historical $500.00)           |
| Finalizing Transfer   (Txn 205) -> Commits Version 2 (Writes updated $400.00)          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
Deadlock Detection and Handling
Lock Timeouts: All transaction lock requests use a hard timeout of 3,000ms.
Deadlock Detection Graph: The database engine maintains an internal Directed Graph tracking active locks and waiting transactions. If a circular wait condition occurs (e.g., Transaction A holds Account 1 and waits for Account 2; Transaction B holds Account 2 and waits for Account 1), the engine identifies the cycle, aborts the younger transaction, rolls back its operations, and returns a serialization failure error for the client application to retry.
